WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:02.310 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  this woman's husband was an inmate at  gotega prison in burundi we've hidden  her face and changed her voice for her  safety  when it caught fire two months ago she  was worried she says she's been to the  prison to try and find out if he  survived but she's not been able to see  him since the guards told her nothing  and sent her away she says another  inmate told her her husband was among  those who burned to death  we don't know how they were buried our  children keep asking me where their  father is i tell them that he died one  of them asked me will you take us to see  his grave when he hears someone calling  me he is asking if it is his father  because he didn't see his funeral or his  body  she's one of many relatives of inmates  who haven't had any official answers  the nearly 100 year old prison was  overcrowded holding three times as many  inmates as it was built for  survivors say one block was badly burned  and the guards refused to unlock the  doors  burundi's vice president visited the  following day he said an electrical  fault started it the government has  since said a report is being prepared  and that 46 inmates died  we spoke to an inmate who says that's  not true  from my sale i could see the bodies  being removed in the morning they  pitched tents and in each tent they  packed 15 to 20 bodies and we counted  there were 20 tens so it's about 300 or  400 a shocking number of dead people the  government declined to comment when we  asked rights groups say the government's  trying to cover up a disaster for which  it's responsible and that it should  investigate and publish the list of  names of those who died  when you have a fire such as this with  with potentially massive death counts  that that goes well beyond uh the  official number uh that's an  embarrassment it it it reflects the  reality which is that um there is a  there is a systemic issue with  incarceration in burundi  this man says he wants to know what  happened to his brother who was an  inmate but he fears being jailed or  killed himself  we cannot continue asking questions for  the sake of our safety asking again is  like a criminal offence we have to keep  quiet  he's from one of dozens maybe hundreds  of bereaved families seeking closure but  are too scared to even ask what happened  to their loved ones malcolm webb al  jazeera

